QUESTIONS = [
    {"id": "q-notation", "at": {"node": "sect-2.2"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": r"Which Hilbert space is $\rho_\text{NB}=\mathbb{1}$ the identity on, and what do $O(1)$ and $O(1/G)$ refer to?",
     "a": r"The auxiliary space $\mathcal{H}_0$ holds all boundary data on a slice; the physical space $\mathcal{H}$ identifies data related by diffeomorphisms, including evolution by $H_\text{WDW}$. As an operator on $\mathcal{H}_0$, $\rho_\text{NB}=\delta(H_\text{WDW})$, which is the identity on $\mathcal{H}$. With horizons, $\mathbb{1}_\mathcal{M}$ is the identity on the observer's algebra in background $\mathcal{M}$. $O(1/G)$ is the leading, state-independent entropy, $\log\mathcal{Z}'_\mathcal{M}$; $O(1)$ is what perturbative algebras compute.",
     "answeredBy": ["sect-2.2-p2", "sec-canonical_quant-p5", "section-4.1-p4"]},
    {"id": "q-no-hh", "at": {"node": "sect-2.1"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": "Why does the usual Hartle–Hawking geometry not contribute?",
     "a": r"The observer's worldline is a conserved particle line and cannot just stop. On a geometry that caps off in the past of a single slice, the worldline entering from that slice has nowhere to end. Once bra and ket are both specified, it can run from one to the other through a connected geometry. So with an observer the disconnected, two-hemisphere contribution is absent altogether, not merely subleading.",
     "answeredBy": ["sect-2.1-p2", "sect-1.intro-p7"]},
    {"id": "q-identity-entropy", "at": {"node": "sect-2.2"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": "How can an unnormalized identity operator define an entropy?",
     "a": r"Through relative entropy. For a normalized state, $S_\text{vN}(\rho)=-S_\text{rel}(\rho\|\mathbb{1})$, and rescaling the reference by $\lambda$ shifts the entropy by $\log\lambda$. The no-boundary state is the identity with a definite normalization, its path-integral norm, about $e^{-I}$ with a dominant saddle. So $S_\text{vN}(\rho)=-S_\text{rel}(\rho\|\tilde\rho_\text{NB})-I$, and the constant is fixed rather than arbitrary.",
     "answeredBy": ["sect-2.2-p6", "sect-1.intro-p5"]},
    {"id": "q-lapse", "at": {"node": "sec-canonical_quant"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": "Why integrate the lapse over both signs, when the Euclidean path integral seems to use only positive lapse?",
     "a": r"Integrating $N$ over the whole real line gives $\delta(H_\text{WDW})$, a Hermitian operator, and treats bra and ket alike: geometries in which either boundary comes first both count. In JT gravity two classical solutions connect bra and ket, one for each time order. The paper shows explicitly that the Lorentzian and Euclidean path integrals agree in AdS JT gravity, with and without an observer.",
     "answeredBy": ["sec-canonical_quant-p3", "sec-canonical_quant-p4", "subsect-JT1empty-p4"]},
    {"id": "q-topology", "at": {"node": "sect-2.4topo"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": "Does summing over topologies change the result?",
     "a": r"Not the argument: inner products also sum over all topologies without extra boundaries, so $\rho_\text{NB}$ remains the identity. What changes is the Hilbert space. Without the recent rules for observers, closed universes would have a one-dimensional Hilbert space. In JT gravity the observer's state becomes the identity on an $N$-dimensional space spanned by the energy eigenstates of one ensemble member, while without an observer the space is one-dimensional.",
     "answeredBy": ["sect-2.4topo-p2", "sect-2.4topo-p3", "subsect-JT4nonpert-p2"]},
    {"id": "q-jt-adds", "at": {"node": "sect-3.3ONBJT"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": "What does the JT calculation add beyond the general argument?",
     "a": r"An exact check. The Euclidean wormhole amplitudes, computed independently in earlier work, are reproduced by inserting the identity operator built canonically from the Wheeler–DeWitt and momentum constraints. One by-product is new: the Euclidean path integral imposes the momentum constraint as well, which earlier calculations of this amplitude did not make manifest. With an observer the check uses asymptotic boundaries only, since the finite-cutoff amplitude is unknown.",
     "answeredBy": ["sect-3.3ONBJT-p1", "sect-3.3ONBJT-p3", "sect-3.3ONBJT-p4"]},
    {"id": "q-sphere", "at": {"node": "section-4.3"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": "With a horizon the leading topology is a sphere, yet globally there is no sphere. How do these fit?",
     "a": r"Globally the observer's worldline forbids the sphere, and the state is a bra-ket wormhole. Reducing to the observer's diamond, by tracing out the unseen region, the path integral generally does include a sphere, as in de Sitter's static patch. The paper calls the resulting equality confusing and non-perturbative: understanding it would require splitting the global Hilbert space into subregion Hilbert spaces non-perturbatively, which it leaves open, suggesting JT gravity with $\Lambda>0$ as a test.",
     "answeredBy": ["section-4.3-p7", "sect-1.intro-p9"]},
    {"id": "q-area", "at": {"node": "section-4.3"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": "Is the leading entropy always the horizon area over $4G$?",
     "a": r"No. The leading term is $\log\mathcal{Z}'_\mathcal{M}$, minus the on-shell action of the dominant no-boundary saddle. In de Sitter that equals $A/4G$, and for regions bounded by extremal surfaces it should agree with area-based traces. For other regions the paper does not expect the area. In the axion-supported FLRW example of its discussion, $-I$ and the horizon area indeed differ.",
     "answeredBy": ["section-4.3-p5", "section-4.1-p4", "sect-5.conclusion-p5"]},
    {"id": "q-nokms", "at": {"node": "sect-nogo"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": "Does the no-KMS theorem undermine the claim that the no-boundary state is a trace?",
     "a": r"The paper says not. The theorem shows that de Sitter's explicit construction of the trace, which uses a state that is KMS on the worldline, cannot be repeated on other asymptotically de Sitter backgrounds, and that the resulting entropy cannot be an equilibrium thermodynamic entropy. It does not show that the no-boundary state fails to be a trace. The paper suggests that a weaker “weak KMS” property of no-boundary saddles may suffice.",
     "answeredBy": ["sect-nogo-p1", "sect-5.conclusion-p2"]},
    {"id": "q-not-shown", "at": {"node": "sect-5.conclusion"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": "What does the paper not show?",
     "a": r"The general argument is formal: it assumes a well-defined integration contour, and topology change has no Hamiltonian description. The exact check is in two-dimensional JT gravity with complete access. For observers with horizons, no example beyond de Sitter is worked out, a type III algebra without trace is not excluded, and the sphere's appearance on reduction is not understood. In the axion FLRW example it is unclear which saddle dominates.",
     "answeredBy": ["sect-2.1-p3", "sect-2.4topo-p1", "sect-5.conclusion-p2", "sect-5.conclusion-p6"]},
    {"id": "q-qubits", "at": {"node": "section-4.2"}, "source": "ai", "status": "draft",
     "q": "What does the qubit model illustrate?",
     "a": r"How an unnormalizable identity can still carry finite information. The identity on $N$ left and $N$ right qubits, restricted to the right, is $2^N$ times the maximally mixed right state, a tracial state. As $N\to\infty$ the prefactor diverges, like $\mathcal{Z}'_\mathcal{M}$ with $N$ playing $1/G$, while the remaining state stays finite, modelling a type II$_1$ algebra; a direct sum of sectors gives each its own prefactor.",
     "answeredBy": ["section-4.2-p1", "section-4.2-p2", "section-4.2-p3"]},
]

BACKGROUND = [
    {"id": "bg-types", "after": "sect-1.intro-p4", "title": "Types of von Neumann algebras and traces", "audience": ["physicist", "student"],
     "body": r"""<p>A von Neumann algebra is an algebra of bounded operators closed under adjoints and weak limits; the operators of a subsystem or a spacetime region form one. Murray and von Neumann sorted the factors, algebras with trivial center, into three types. Type I is the familiar case, all operators on some Hilbert space: there are pure states, a trace, and entropy is defined absolutely. Type III, the algebra of a region in quantum field theory, has no trace, and entanglement entropy diverges. Type II lies between: there is a trace, unique up to rescaling, so density matrices and entropies exist, but no pure states, and rescaling the trace shifts every entropy by the same constant. Type II$_1$ has a normalizable maximum-entropy state; type II$_\infty$ does not. Gravitational observer algebras are type II, so they fix entropy differences but leave this constant open, which the no-boundary state is meant to fix.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-clpw", "after": "sect-1.intro-p4", "title": "The de Sitter static patch with an observer", "audience": ["physicist", "student"],
     "body": r"""<p>In de Sitter space an eternal observer sees only the static patch, a causal diamond bounded by a cosmological horizon. Quantum fields in the patch alone form a type III algebra. Gravity demands that operators commute with the patch's boost generator, which includes the observer's energy $q\ge0$, so fields are dressed to the observer's clock. Chandrasekaran, Longo, Penington and Witten showed that the resulting static patch algebra is type II$_1$. Its trace is the Bunch–Davies state of the fields together with a thermal distribution of the observer's energy, the maximum-entropy state. Entropies computed with this trace agree with the generalized entropy of the patch, area over $4G$ plus field entropy, up to a state-independent constant. The paper's claim is that the no-boundary path integral, the Euclidean sphere, prepares this trace with its normalization, $e^{A/4G}$, included.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-nb", "after": "sect-2.1-p2", "title": "The Hartle–Hawking no-boundary proposal", "audience": ["student"],
     "body": r"""<p>The Hartle–Hawking no-boundary proposal holds that the wavefunction of the universe on a spatial slice is the gravitational path integral over geometries whose only boundary is that slice. The universe has no initial boundary: it caps off smoothly, typically through a Euclidean region. For de Sitter space the dominant geometry is half a Euclidean four-sphere glued to Lorentzian de Sitter, and its action gives the wavefunction a weight $e^{A/8G}$, half the de Sitter entropy. The norm, a full sphere, then gives $e^{A/4G}$. Contours of integration, the sign of the lapse, and which complex saddles to include are long-standing debates. A density matrix can be prepared in the same way, with both bra and ket slices as boundaries; it then also receives contributions from connected bra-ket geometries. The paper's point is that an observer removes the capped geometry altogether, leaving only the connected ones.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-constraints", "after": "sect-2.2-p2", "title": "Constraints and the physical Hilbert space", "audience": ["physicist", "student"],
     "body": r"""<p>In a gauge theory the Hamiltonian contains constraints, generators of gauge transformations that must vanish on physical states. Dirac's prescription is to impose them as $C|\psi\rangle=0$. When the constraint has continuous spectrum, as the Wheeler–DeWitt constraint $H_\text{WDW}$ of gravity does, such solutions are not normalizable in the auxiliary Hilbert space of all boundary data, so a new inner product is needed. Group averaging, or the rigging map, provides it: average over the gauge orbit, $\eta=\int dN\,e^{-iNH}/2\pi=\delta(H)$, and define $\langle\langle\psi'|\psi\rangle\rangle=\langle\psi'|\eta|\psi\rangle$. Equivalently, states differing by $H|\phi\rangle$ are identified, and gauge fixing gives a Klein–Gordon inner product on superspace. Because $H_\text{WDW}$ generates time evolution, data on two slices of one geometry are the same physical state. The paper identifies the no-boundary density matrix with $\eta$ itself.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-relent", "after": "sect-2.2-p6", "title": "Relative entropy and the identity", "audience": ["student"],
     "body": r"""<p>For density matrices $\rho$ and $\sigma$, the relative entropy is $S_\text{rel}(\rho\|\sigma)=\operatorname{Tr}\rho\log\rho-\operatorname{Tr}\rho\log\sigma$. It is non-negative when both are normalized and vanishes only for $\rho=\sigma$. Taking $\sigma=\mathbb{1}$, an unnormalized operator, the second term vanishes and $S_\text{rel}(\rho\|\mathbb{1})=-S_\text{vN}(\rho)$. If instead $\sigma=\lambda\mathbb{1}$, the second term is $\log\lambda$, so every entropy is shifted by the same constant. Relative entropy has a definition, due to Araki, for any von Neumann algebra, even where $\operatorname{Tr}\rho\log\rho$ makes no sense, as in quantum field theory. So entropy can be defined relative to the identity, provided one knows which operator, with which normalization, plays the identity. That normalization is the constant left open by type II algebras, and the no-boundary state, with its path-integral norm $e^{-I}$, supplies it.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-jt", "after": "sect-4.JT-p2", "title": "JT gravity, trumpets and wormholes", "audience": ["student"],
     "body": r"""<p>Jackiw–Teitelboim gravity is two-dimensional gravity coupled to a scalar dilaton $\Phi$. Integrating out $\Phi$ fixes the curvature, $R=-2$ for negative cosmological constant, so all geometries are locally hyperbolic, and the remaining dynamics live at boundaries. With an asymptotic boundary of renormalized length $\beta$, that dynamics is the Schwarzian theory, with spectral density $\rho(E)$. A geometry between an asymptotic boundary and a closed geodesic of length $b$ is a trumpet, with partition function $e^{-b^2/4\beta}/\sqrt{2\pi\beta}$. Gluing two trumpets along the geodesic, integrated with measure $b\,db$, gives the double-trumpet wormhole connecting two boundaries. Summing over all topologies, Saad, Shenker and Stanford showed, reproduces a random-matrix integral. In Lorentzian signature the same geometries describe closed universes that bang and crunch, labelled by their maximal size $b$, which is why wormhole amplitudes can be compared with canonical closed-universe states.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-kms", "after": "sect-nogo-p2", "title": "The KMS condition and thermal states", "audience": ["physicist", "student"],
     "body": r"""<p>A Gibbs state $e^{-\beta H}/Z$ satisfies $\operatorname{Tr}(e^{-\beta H}A\,B(t))=\operatorname{Tr}(e^{-\beta H}B(t+i\beta)A)$, by cyclicity of the trace. The Kubo–Martin–Schwinger condition keeps this relation, together with analyticity of correlators in the strip $0<\operatorname{Im}t<\beta$, as the definition of thermal equilibrium; it still makes sense in infinite systems where $e^{-\beta H}$ is not trace-class. Tomita–Takesaki theory shows that any faithful state is KMS with respect to its own modular flow, at $\beta=1$. The physical content is therefore that the modular flow be a geometric time translation. In de Sitter space the Bunch–Davies state is KMS with respect to static-patch time at $\beta=2\pi/H$, since the patch has a boost-like Killing symmetry. CLPW used exactly this to construct the trace for an observer, which is why the paper asks whether other cosmologies admit such a state.</p>"""},
]
